# Behavioral test for the automatic `'actor` -> `'atomic` promotion pass (Part 2 of
# the `'atomic` design — see docs/qualifiers.md's `'atomic` section and
# src/transpiler/promote_atomic.rs). `counter` here is `'actor`-qualified, never
# escapes its local scope, is never used inside a `with` block, and every access
# decomposes into a single recognized atomic primitive — so the promotion pass
# rewrites it to the `'atomic` representation (Arc<AtomicIsize> / Rc<Cell<isize>>)
# instead of Arc<Mutex<isize>> / Rc<RefCell<isize>>.
#
# Also proves a real, previously-broken combination now works: a scalar `'actor`
# binding's compound assignment (`counter += 5`) used to transpile to a bare `+=`
# directly on the `Arc<Mutex<isize>>` wrapper with no `.lock()` at all — not valid
# Rust. Promoting to `'atomic` sidesteps that bug entirely by using `fetch_add`
# instead.
#
# `tests/atomic_qualifier.rs`'s `promotion_fires_for_local_scalar_actor` inspects
# the actual generated Rust (not just this file's stdout) to confirm the atomic
# representation is really what gets emitted, not just that the output happens to
# match — see that test for the codegen-level half of this regression pin.
def main():
    var counter'actor = 0
    counter += 5
    counter -= 2
    print "{counter}"
